index 7b8f29c0d6..ed719881d4 100644
--- a/docs/config.md
+++ b/docs/config.md
@@ -107,7 +107,7 @@ ## connections.toml
 ```toml
 [[connection]]
 name     = "local"                     # required, unique; no / or \, not starting with .
-engine   = "postgres"                  # postgres | mysql | sqlite | mssql | redis
+engine   = "postgres"                  # postgres | gaussdb | mysql | sqlite | mssql | redis
 dsn      = "postgres://me@localhost:5432/app?sslmode=disable"
 password_cmd = "security find-generic-password -s sqlmux-local -w"
 read_only = false                      # refuse writes on this connection
@@ -123,6 +123,7 @@ ## connections.toml
 | engine | dsn |
 |---|---|
 | `postgres` | `postgres://user@host:5432/db?sslmode=disable` (the tree lists the other databases on the server too) |
+| `gaussdb` | `gaussdb://user@host:8000/db?sslmode=prefer`; also `opengauss://`, `postgres://`, `postgresql://`, or keyword/value form under this explicit engine |
 | `mysql` | `user@tcp(host:3306)/db` or `mysql://user@host:3306/db` |
 | `sqlite` | a file path; `~` is expanded, `?mode=ro` and other query parameters pass through; a missing file is not created |
 | `mssql` | `sqlserver://user@host:1433?database=app` (`mssql://` works too); driver options such as `encrypt` pass through. A server with a self-signed certificate, like the official Docker image, needs `&TrustServerCertificate=true` |
@@ -150,6 +151,33 @@ ## connections.toml
 
 `ssh = "user@host"` runs `ssh -W host:port user@host` for each connection; the port, key and `ProxyJump` come from `~/.ssh/config`, and no local port is opened. ssh cannot ask for a password or passphrase here, so `ssh-add` the key first. It does not apply to SQLite or to connections given on the command line.
 
+### GaussDB and openGauss
+
+Use `engine = "gaussdb"` for PostgreSQL-protocol GaussDB or openGauss. GaussDB(for MySQL) uses `engine = "mysql"`. The native Go driver supports GaussDB SHA256, MD5 and PostgreSQL SCRAM authentication; GaussDB SHA256 is a different protocol from SCRAM-SHA-256. SM3 authentication is not supported by this driver. It does not load JDBC driver jars.
+
+```toml
+[[connection]]
+name         = "gauss"
+engine       = "gaussdb"
+dsn          = "gaussdb://app_user@db.example:8000/app?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/path/to/ca.pem"
+password_env = "GAUSS_APP_PASSWORD"
+databases    = ["app"]
+schemas      = ["app.public"]
+```
+
+Use the actual instance port. `sslmode`, `sslrootcert`, `sslcert` and `sslkey` are handled by the native driver. Password sources, SSH tunnels, database/schema selection, completion, editing, manual transactions and CSV/JSON export work as for other SQL connections. The native driver reads `GAUSSDB_HOST`, `GAUSSDB_PORT`, `GAUSSDB_DATABASE`, `GAUSSDB_USER`, `GAUSSDB_PASSWORD`, `GAUSSDB_PASSFILE` and other `GAUSSDB_*` connection variables; it does not use PostgreSQL's `PG*` variables.
+
+For an existing JDBC URL such as `jdbc:postgresql://db.example:30100/app`, remove `jdbc:` and use that URL as the DSN with explicit `engine = "gaussdb"`. On the command line, use `gaussdb://` or `opengauss://` to select the GaussDB driver; `postgres://` selects PostgreSQL.
+
+The engine uses GaussDB catalogs for generated columns, partitions, keys, indexes and function definitions. SQL follows the database's own compatibility mode: for example, an Oracle-compatible database can treat an empty string as NULL. sqlmux preserves the server's returned values.
+
+Integration tests use `SQLMUX_TEST_GAUSSDB`, independently of the PostgreSQL instance. Set it only to a disposable test database whose account can create schemas, tables, types, functions, sequences and indexes. The tests create and clean up their own schemas. The INCLUDE-index tests require an instance started with `enable_ustore=on`; materialized views use Astore.
+
+```sh
+SQLMUX_TEST_GAUSSDB='gaussdb://user:password@localhost:55433/postgres?sslmode=disable' \
+  go test -race -tags integration ./internal/db/gaussdb
+```
+
 ### External commands (`engine = "exec"`)
 
 For a database you can only reach through a command-line tool, sqlmux can run every statement through that command instead of a driver. Such a connection is always read-only, and is written by hand (the connection form and manager don't list it).
